      "detail": "At the island’s north-east tip a spur of turf runs out from the foghorn to a deep cleft in the cliffs. In any swell the sea surges into it with a boom like a slammed door, and the spray comes up over the top. A waist-high stone wall runs along the edge, with a sign: ‘DANGER — THE CHURN — KEEP BEHIND THE WALL’.\n\nOn the far side of the wall the turf is torn at the lip, as if something heavy went over. The police never came up here: they were looking at a boat.\n\nA fingerpost stands at the end of the spur: ‘Lighthouse 3 min · Chapel 12 min (cliff path) · Landing 16 min (main path)’. The spur is a dead end. The only ways back are the cliff path, south along the east side, and the main path past the lighthouse."
